          "heading": "Deep dive and misconception repair",
          "content": "## Deep dive and misconception repair\n\n### Modeling clinic: the missing connection\nA three-dimensional building component can be drawn very neatly while still representing the wrong mechanical system. The typical difficulty is not solving the equations but specifying where the applied force enters and where the reactions leave. Consider a steel beam seated on a masonry wall. A textbook roller might allow unrestricted horizontal motion, but the real bearing can have friction and a grout bed. The correct model may depend on whether the question concerns gravity deflection, horizontal thermal movement, or seismic displacement. Each investigation can demand a different support idealization. Therefore write the physical load path in words before selecting mathematical boundary conditions.\n\nA free-body diagram should include all externally applied actions on the isolated body, including any applied couples, and should not include internal forces acting entirely within that body. When the system is cut at a joint, each newly exposed interface must be replaced by a compatible force and possible moment. A pin-jointed truss cut exposes axial members; a rigid frame cut generally exposes axial force, shear and bending. If the free-body diagram of a frame member contains only a guessed horizontal force, the internal moment equilibrium may be impossible to satisfy.\n\nA structural system may be externally supported but internally unstable. For example, a four-bar rectangular pin-jointed framework without diagonal bracing can distort into a parallelogram without member extension in the ideal kinematic picture. Adding more reaction constraints at its base does not necessarily prevent the internal distortion. This demonstrates why the count of reaction components and the count of bar members must be interpreted along with geometry.\n\n#### A falsification exercise\nStart with the assertion that a support is a frictionless roller. Ask what observed behavior would disprove it. If the support has corrosion, a locking device or a horizontal guide, resistance may develop. If uplift is possible, a bearing may lose contact and cease to impose a constraint. Suppose the beam is being checked only under a permanent vertical action. You may still use a pinned-roller model for a first-pass vertical response provided you document the simplification, explain its likely effect and do not use the result to make decisions about the bearing itself.\n\nModel classification should follow degrees of freedom, not visual labels. A two-dimensional beam node can translate in x and y and rotate about the perpendicular axis. A vertical roller removes the y displacement while permitting x and rotation. A pin removes x and y but permits rotation. A fixed connection removes all three. A rigid member joining these nodes transfers actions according to its stiffness; a truss bar constrains axial elongation but has no ideal bending stiffness.\n\nFor systems with internal releases, conduct the stability test after each release. It is possible to make a determinate base model from an indeterminate system by releasing one redundant and also to accidentally convert the system into a mechanism. The proposed release must preserve enough restraints and connectivity for the primary structure to withstand the original load configuration. This point becomes central when the force method is introduced.\n\nDimensional analysis gives an immediate independent test. Force equilibrium has units of N or kN; moment equilibrium has units of N\u00b7m or kN\u00b7m. A surface pressure in kN/m\u00b2 multiplied by tributary width in m gives a beam line load in kN/m. Multiplying it by the girder span as well gives total resultant force in kN. Entering a total force as a line intensity will produce a different scale of response even though a computer may process it without complaint."
