          "id": "sec-2-4",
          "secNumber": "2.4",
          "heading": "Deep dive and misconception repair",
          "content": "## Deep dive and misconception repair\n\n### Diagram clinic: discontinuities and extrema\nConsider a simply supported beam with a point force positioned away from midspan and a full-span distributed load. A useful hand solution begins with reactions and then writes piecewise expressions for shear and moment. The shear to the left of the point action differs from the shear to its right by exactly the downward point-force magnitude. The bending moment is continuous through that location if no concentrated couple is applied. If a numerical chart instead shows a moment jump at the point-force coordinate, its implementation likely conflates force with couple.\n\nThe maximum positive moment need not occur where the largest force is applied. In regions where shear is positive, moment grows with x. Where shear is negative, moment decreases with x. A point-load jump in shear may cross zero abruptly, creating a local moment maximum at the load coordinate. Otherwise one can solve V(x)=0 in each smooth region and compare the resulting candidate moments. This logic generalizes to piecewise load functions and avoids blind reliance on sampled graphs.\n\nDraw the distributed load and the beam's resultant separately. When computing reactions, a uniform line load can be replaced by a single resultant at the centroid, because global equilibrium depends on the resultant and its moment. When calculating internal shear and moment as functions of x, however, the replacement cannot be used as if it were a point force acting at all partial beam cuts. A cut at x encloses only the portion of distributed load to its left; the correct force is wx, not the total wL.\n\nFor a beam with a pure end couple, the bending-moment boundary conditions differ from an unloaded pin. The internal diagram can include a nonzero end moment consistent with the applied couple, despite a pin support that cannot resist a couple. The statement 'moment at a pin equals zero' applies to the reaction transmitted by a pin, not to an arbitrary applied couple at the same coordinate. This distinction often prevents an apparently contradictory free-body diagram.\n\n#### Sensitivity study: two independent scalings\nIf a beam carries only a constant uniform load w and is geometrically similar as its span changes, the total load increases with L. The support reactions each equal wL/2. The maximum sagging moment equals wL\u00b2/8, located at midspan. With constant E and I, the maximum deflection equals 5wL\u2074/(384EI). Doubling span while keeping w, E and I fixed multiplies reaction by two, bending moment by four and deflection by sixteen. This is why checking only strength can overlook serviceability for a long slender span.\n\nIf instead the total uniform load W is held constant while changing span, the line intensity is w=W/L. Reactions remain W/2, maximum moment scales as WL/8 and deflection scales as 5WL\u00b3/(384EI). The two cases answer different engineering questions; mixing their scaling conclusions is a common conceptual error. Always state whether total action or distributed intensity is held fixed.\n\nThe simple-beam deflection formula is derived under small curvature, constant stiffness, linear elastic material and supports that prevent vertical displacement but not rotation. In practice, shear flexibility can matter for deep beams, supports can settle, and concrete stiffness may vary after cracking. The correct use of a closed form is to understand the mechanism and assess whether its assumptions are adequate, not to disguise those assumptions behind several decimal places."
