          "id": "sec-3-5",
          "secNumber": "3.5",
          "heading": "Multi-case calculations and validation",
          "content": "## Multi-case calculations and validation\n\n### Case 3.1: Reaction shift\n\nA symmetric triangular truss with span 6.0 m, apex rise 1.5 m and downward apex load 28 kN has diagonal angle theta=arctan(2h/L)=26.57\u00b0. Each inclined bar carries compression magnitude P/(2 sin theta)=31.305 kN; the bottom tie carries tension P/(2tan theta)=28.000 kN. Increasing rise at fixed span lowers those member-force magnitudes. This exact equilibrium result assumes pin joints, symmetric geometry and a load applied at the apex only; off-center deck loading requires a different joint-load pattern.\n\n**Verification challenge:** Check the key result above against a limiting case in which one action is reduced to zero, or one geometric parameter is increased. For pin-jointed trusses and displacement, identify the response that changes most and state the governing physical mechanism. Do not use this case as a final capacity evaluation; it omits detailed component resistance and code-required factors.\n\n### Case 3.2: Longer girder\n\nA symmetric triangular truss with span 9.0 m, apex rise 1.9 m and downward apex load 34 kN has diagonal angle theta=arctan(2h/L)=22.89\u00b0. Each inclined bar carries compression magnitude P/(2 sin theta)=43.705 kN; the bottom tie carries tension P/(2tan theta)=40.263 kN. Increasing rise at fixed span lowers those member-force magnitudes. This exact equilibrium result assumes pin joints, symmetric geometry and a load applied at the apex only; off-center deck loading requires a different joint-load pattern.\n\n**Verification challenge:** Check the key result above against a limiting case in which one action is reduced to zero, or one geometric parameter is increased. For pin-jointed trusses and displacement, identify the response that changes most and state the governing physical mechanism. Do not use this case as a final capacity evaluation; it omits detailed component resistance and code-required factors.\n\n### Case 3.3: Footbridge loading\n\nA symmetric triangular truss with span 12.0 m, apex rise 2.3 m and downward apex load 41 kN has diagonal angle theta=arctan(2h/L)=20.97\u00b0. Each inclined bar carries compression magnitude P/(2 sin theta)=57.273 kN; the bottom tie carries tension P/(2tan theta)=53.478 kN. Increasing rise at fixed span lowers those member-force magnitudes. This exact equilibrium result assumes pin joints, symmetric geometry and a load applied at the apex only; off-center deck loading requires a different joint-load pattern.\n\n**Verification challenge:** Check the key result above against a limiting case in which one action is reduced to zero, or one geometric parameter is increased. For pin-jointed trusses and displacement, identify the response that changes most and state the governing physical mechanism. Do not use this case as a final capacity evaluation; it omits detailed component resistance and code-required factors.\n\n### Case 3.4: Short lintel\n\nA symmetric triangular truss with span 4.5 m, apex rise 2.7 m and downward apex load 15 kN has diagonal angle theta=arctan(2h/L)=50.19\u00b0. Each inclined bar carries compression magnitude P/(2 sin theta)=9.763 kN; the bottom tie carries tension P/(2tan theta)=6.250 kN. Increasing rise at fixed span lowers those member-force magnitudes. This exact equilibrium result assumes pin joints, symmetric geometry and a load applied at the apex only; off-center deck loading requires a different joint-load pattern.\n\n**Verification challenge:** Check the key result above against a limiting case in which one action is reduced to zero, or one geometric parameter is increased. For pin-jointed trusses and displacement, identify the response that changes most and state the governing physical mechanism. Do not use this case as a final capacity evaluation; it omits detailed component resistance and code-required factors.\n\n### Case 3.5: Beam with equipment\n\nA symmetric triangular truss with span 8.0 m, apex rise 3.1 m and downward apex load 24 kN has diagonal angle theta=arctan(2h/L)=37.78\u00b0. Each inclined bar carries compression magnitude P/(2 sin theta)=19.590 kN; the bottom tie carries tension P/(2tan theta)=15.484 kN. Increasing rise at fixed span lowers those member-force magnitudes. This exact equilibrium result assumes pin joints, symmetric geometry and a load applied at the apex only; off-center deck loading requires a different joint-load pattern.\n\n**Verification challenge:** Check the key result above against a limiting case in which one action is reduced to zero, or one geometric parameter is increased. For pin-jointed trusses and displacement, identify the response that changes most and state the governing physical mechanism. Do not use this case as a final capacity evaluation; it omits detailed component resistance and code-required factors.\n\n### Case 3.6: Symmetric midspan\n\nA symmetric triangular truss with span 10.0 m, apex rise 3.5 m and downward apex load 45 kN has diagonal angle theta=arctan(2h/L)=34.99\u00b0. Each inclined bar carries compression magnitude P/(2 sin theta)=39.235 kN; the bottom tie carries tension P/(2tan theta)=32.143 kN. Increasing rise at fixed span lowers those member-force magnitudes. This exact equilibrium result assumes pin joints, symmetric geometry and a load applied at the apex only; off-center deck loading requires a different joint-load pattern.\n\n**Verification challenge:** Check the key result above against a limiting case in which one action is reduced to zero, or one geometric parameter is increased. For pin-jointed trusses and displacement, identify the response that changes most and state the governing physical mechanism. Do not use this case as a final capacity evaluation; it omits detailed component resistance and code-required factors.\n\n### Case 3.7: Warehouse girder\n\nA symmetric triangular truss with span 7.5 m, apex rise 3.9 m and downward apex load 38 kN has diagonal angle theta=arctan(2h/L)=46.12\u00b0. Each inclined bar carries compression magnitude P/(2 sin theta)=26.358 kN; the bottom tie carries tension P/(2tan theta)=18.269 kN. Increasing rise at fixed span lowers those member-force magnitudes. This exact equilibrium result assumes pin joints, symmetric geometry and a load applied at the apex only; off-center deck loading requires a different joint-load pattern.\n\n**Verification challenge:** Check the key result above against a limiting case in which one action is reduced to zero, or one geometric parameter is increased. For pin-jointed trusses and displacement, identify the response that changes most and state the governing physical mechanism. Do not use this case as a final capacity evaluation; it omits detailed component resistance and code-required factors.\n\n### Case 3.8: Roof beam\n\nA symmetric triangular truss with span 5.5 m, apex rise 4.3 m and downward apex load 19 kN has diagonal angle theta=arctan(2h/L)=57.40\u00b0. Each inclined bar carries compression magnitude P/(2 sin theta)=11.277 kN; the bottom tie carries tension P/(2tan theta)=6.076 kN. Increasing rise at fixed span lowers those member-force magnitudes. This exact equilibrium result assumes pin joints, symmetric geometry and a load applied at the apex only; off-center deck loading requires a different joint-load pattern.\n\n**Verification challenge:** Check the key result above against a limiting case in which one action is reduced to zero, or one geometric parameter is increased. For pin-jointed trusses and displacement, identify the response that changes most and state the governing physical mechanism. Do not use this case as a final capacity evaluation; it omits detailed component resistance and code-required factors."
