          "heading": "Deep dive and misconception repair",
          "content": "## Deep dive and misconception repair\n\n### Curved systems clinic: an arch is not an inverted cable in every respect\nA three-hinged arch and a hanging cable can share a parabolic appearance but resist loads by fundamentally different mechanisms. The arch is a compression-dominated structural element that can also carry shear and bending. A perfectly flexible cable carries tension only and must adopt a geometry compatible with the applied loading. A cable of fixed geometry under changed loading can redistribute shape substantially, whereas the first-order arch analysis generally holds its centerline geometry fixed.\n\nThe three-hinged arch's crown condition provides a particularly transparent force solution. First calculate reactions for an equivalent simply supported beam under the same vertical loading. Next evaluate beam bending moment at the crown. Because the arch has an internal hinge with zero transmitted moment, the horizontal thrust multiplied by rise must cancel the equivalent beam moment at that location. This gives H=Mbeam(crown)/f. Finally calculate any other section's arch moment by subtracting H times the arch ordinate from the equivalent beam moment.\n\nThis relation illustrates a geometric trade-off. A shallow arch has small rise f. If the equivalent crown moment is fixed, horizontal thrust must be large. More rise can reduce thrust but may increase overall height, change clearance and alter member lengths. Neither choice can be judged from the thrust formula alone. Soil capacity, footing restraint, construction sequence, environmental actions and material stability all matter in design practice.\n\n#### Load mismatch and arch bending\nFor a parabolic arch under a full-span uniform load per horizontal length, an ideal funicular compression line can align well with the arch geometry, producing low bending when supports and loading match the assumptions. A concentrated off-center load destroys that exact match, leaving nonzero bending even though crown moment is constrained to zero. This distinction explains why a geometry that seems efficient for one load case may still experience flexure under real varying traffic or asymmetric snow.\n\nThe cable equation H=wL\u00b2/(8f) requires that w be uniform per horizontal projected distance. If w is instead a constant self-weight per cable arc length, the exact equilibrium shape is a catenary. In a shallow cable the parabolic approximation may be close, but the difference becomes relevant for large sag. One must not use the parabolic formula as a general exact solution for any cable material or loading.\n\nSupport horizontal action has an engineering consequence: foundations must supply the balancing thrust or an explicit tension tie must carry it. A three-hinged arch with a tie is not mechanically the same as an untied arch thrusting into the abutments. The tie changes the global force path. When presenting a computed H, label it 'horizontal thrust in this idealized arch' rather than 'safe foundation load'."
