          "id": "sec-6-4",
          "secNumber": "6.4",
          "heading": "Deep dive and misconception repair",
          "content": "## Deep dive and misconception repair\n\n### Influence clinic: where the load is and what response is measured\nAn influence line should be named by its response coordinate. 'Moment at section c as a function of wheel position z' has a clear meaning; 'the beam's moment influence line' without specifying a section does not. This precision separates the response function from the variable moving action. It also explains why a simple-beam influence line for a support reaction is triangular while the influence line for a moment at an interior section has two linear branches meeting at that section.\n\nFor a beam of span L, let a unit force move to z. The left reaction is (L-z)/L. At a section c to the left of z, the local bending moment equals that reaction times c. At a section c to the right of z, subtract the force times (c-z). Simplification gives the two branches of the moment influence function. The peak ordinate for a positive unit load occurs at z=c and equals c(L-c)/L. This result does not depend on EI because the structure is statically determinate.\n\nWhen a two-axle vehicle moves across the span, each axle contributes its force times the ordinate under its own position. The two loads have a fixed spacing, so their positions are not independent variables. A search that moves each axle separately into its own favorable location invents an impossible vehicle configuration and overestimates the combined maximum. The optimization must preserve axle spacing, axle order and the restriction that forces outside the span make no contribution.\n\n#### Fine-grid scanning is approximate\nA spreadsheet or browser simulation can scan positions in small increments and retain the largest computed response. This is reliable as an exploratory numerical method if its resolution and scope are reported. If the grid step is too large, it may miss a peak occurring when one axle aligns exactly with the observed section. A useful improvement is to evaluate the positions at which an axle crosses the section, an axle enters or exits the span, and any stationary points of a smooth response region.\n\nAn envelope shows the maximum permitted response at each structural coordinate after considering the entire set of allowed vehicle positions. The line drawn through envelope values generally does not correspond to any single vehicle placement. It is therefore incorrect to interpret an envelope as an instantaneous bending-moment diagram. Negative shear envelopes require separate treatment since peak positive and peak negative values can govern different checks.\n\nReal bridges require additional factors outside this quasistatic demonstration: vehicle lateral distribution, impact or dynamic amplification where applicable, traffic load models, fatigue, composite interaction, local deck action and code requirements. The influence line still provides the conceptual foundation for understanding which positions matter and why a fixed-load sketch is insufficient."
