          "id": "sec-7-5",
          "secNumber": "7.5",
          "heading": "Multi-case calculations and validation",
          "content": "## Multi-case calculations and validation\n\n### Case 7.1: Reaction shift\n\nConsider a propped cantilever of length 6.0 m, uniform load 3.1 kN/m and a downward force 28 kN applied at the right support location. With E=200 GPa and I=2.00\u00d710^-4 m^4, zero-settlement upward reaction is P+3wL/8=34.975 kN. If that prop settles downward 4 mm, the elastic compatibility correction is -3EI s/L^3=-2.222 kN, so the predicted reaction becomes 32.753 kN. Contact loss or support uplift would invalidate a bilateral restraint model.\n\n**Verification challenge:** Check the key result above against a limiting case in which one action is reduced to zero, or one geometric parameter is increased. For redundancy, compatibility and force method, identify the response that changes most and state the governing physical mechanism. Do not use this case as a final capacity evaluation; it omits detailed component resistance and code-required factors.\n\n### Case 7.2: Longer girder\n\nConsider a propped cantilever of length 9.0 m, uniform load 2.0 kN/m and a downward force 34 kN applied at the right support location. With E=200 GPa and I=2.20\u00d710^-4 m^4, zero-settlement upward reaction is P+3wL/8=40.750 kN. If that prop settles downward 4 mm, the elastic compatibility correction is -3EI s/L^3=-0.724 kN, so the predicted reaction becomes 40.026 kN. Contact loss or support uplift would invalidate a bilateral restraint model.\n\n**Verification challenge:** Check the key result above against a limiting case in which one action is reduced to zero, or one geometric parameter is increased. For redundancy, compatibility and force method, identify the response that changes most and state the governing physical mechanism. Do not use this case as a final capacity evaluation; it omits detailed component resistance and code-required factors.\n\n### Case 7.3: Footbridge loading\n\nConsider a propped cantilever of length 12.0 m, uniform load 4.8 kN/m and a downward force 41 kN applied at the right support location. With E=200 GPa and I=2.40\u00d710^-4 m^4, zero-settlement upward reaction is P+3wL/8=62.600 kN. If that prop settles downward 4 mm, the elastic compatibility correction is -3EI s/L^3=-0.333 kN, so the predicted reaction becomes 62.267 kN. Contact loss or support uplift would invalidate a bilateral restraint model.\n\n**Verification challenge:** Check the key result above against a limiting case in which one action is reduced to zero, or one geometric parameter is increased. For redundancy, compatibility and force method, identify the response that changes most and state the governing physical mechanism. Do not use this case as a final capacity evaluation; it omits detailed component resistance and code-required factors.\n\n### Case 7.4: Short lintel\n\nConsider a propped cantilever of length 4.5 m, uniform load 1.2 kN/m and a downward force 15 kN applied at the right support location. With E=200 GPa and I=2.60\u00d710^-4 m^4, zero-settlement upward reaction is P+3wL/8=17.025 kN. If that prop settles downward 4 mm, the elastic compatibility correction is -3EI s/L^3=-6.848 kN, so the predicted reaction becomes 10.177 kN. Contact loss or support uplift would invalidate a bilateral restraint model.\n\n**Verification challenge:** Check the key result above against a limiting case in which one action is reduced to zero, or one geometric parameter is increased. For redundancy, compatibility and force method, identify the response that changes most and state the governing physical mechanism. Do not use this case as a final capacity evaluation; it omits detailed component resistance and code-required factors.\n\n### Case 7.5: Beam with equipment\n\nConsider a propped cantilever of length 8.0 m, uniform load 0.9 kN/m and a downward force 24 kN applied at the right support location. With E=200 GPa and I=2.80\u00d710^-4 m^4, zero-settlement upward reaction is P+3wL/8=26.700 kN. If that prop settles downward 4 mm, the elastic compatibility correction is -3EI s/L^3=-1.312 kN, so the predicted reaction becomes 25.387 kN. Contact loss or support uplift would invalidate a bilateral restraint model.\n\n**Verification challenge:** Check the key result above against a limiting case in which one action is reduced to zero, or one geometric parameter is increased. For redundancy, compatibility and force method, identify the response that changes most and state the governing physical mechanism. Do not use this case as a final capacity evaluation; it omits detailed component resistance and code-required factors.\n\n### Case 7.6: Symmetric midspan\n\nConsider a propped cantilever of length 10.0 m, uniform load 2.4 kN/m and a downward force 45 kN applied at the right support location. With E=200 GPa and I=3.00\u00d710^-4 m^4, zero-settlement upward reaction is P+3wL/8=54.000 kN. If that prop settles downward 4 mm, the elastic compatibility correction is -3EI s/L^3=-0.720 kN, so the predicted reaction becomes 53.280 kN. Contact loss or support uplift would invalidate a bilateral restraint model.\n\n**Verification challenge:** Check the key result above against a limiting case in which one action is reduced to zero, or one geometric parameter is increased. For redundancy, compatibility and force method, identify the response that changes most and state the governing physical mechanism. Do not use this case as a final capacity evaluation; it omits detailed component resistance and code-required factors.\n\n### Case 7.7: Warehouse girder\n\nConsider a propped cantilever of length 7.5 m, uniform load 3.8 kN/m and a downward force 38 kN applied at the right support location. With E=200 GPa and I=3.20\u00d710^-4 m^4, zero-settlement upward reaction is P+3wL/8=48.688 kN. If that prop settles downward 4 mm, the elastic compatibility correction is -3EI s/L^3=-1.820 kN, so the predicted reaction becomes 46.867 kN. Contact loss or support uplift would invalidate a bilateral restraint model.\n\n**Verification challenge:** Check the key result above against a limiting case in which one action is reduced to zero, or one geometric parameter is increased. For redundancy, compatibility and force method, identify the response that changes most and state the governing physical mechanism. Do not use this case as a final capacity evaluation; it omits detailed component resistance and code-required factors.\n\n### Case 7.8: Roof beam\n\nConsider a propped cantilever of length 5.5 m, uniform load 2.1 kN/m and a downward force 19 kN applied at the right support location. With E=200 GPa and I=3.40\u00d710^-4 m^4, zero-settlement upward reaction is P+3wL/8=23.331 kN. If that prop settles downward 4 mm, the elastic compatibility correction is -3EI s/L^3=-4.905 kN, so the predicted reaction becomes 18.427 kN. Contact loss or support uplift would invalidate a bilateral restraint model.\n\n**Verification challenge:** Check the key result above against a limiting case in which one action is reduced to zero, or one geometric parameter is increased. For redundancy, compatibility and force method, identify the response that changes most and state the governing physical mechanism. Do not use this case as a final capacity evaluation; it omits detailed component resistance and code-required factors."
